sections.body:
App config holds the name, identifiers, icons, plugins, permissions and more. If several files exist, `app.config.ts` beats `app.config.js`, which beats `app.json`.

A dynamic config receives the static config and returns the final one:

```ts
import { ExpoConfig, ConfigContext } from 'expo/config';

export default ({ config }: ConfigContext): ExpoConfig => ({
  ...config,
  name: process.env.APP_VARIANT === 'preview' ? 'MyApp (Preview)' : 'MyApp',
  slug: 'my-app',
});
```

Values in `extra` are readable at runtime through `Constants.expoConfig.extra`. The docs are explicit: "You should not include any sensitive information in the app config." Run `npx expo config --type public` to see exactly what gets embedded in builds and updates.

A `_layout.tsx` renders a navigator for the files beside it:

```tsx
// src/app/_layout.tsx
import { Stack } from 'expo-router';

export default function RootLayout() {
  return (
    <Stack>
      <Stack.Screen name="(tabs)" options={{ headerShown: false }} />
      <Stack.Screen name="compose" options={{ presentation: 'modal' }} />
    </Stack>
  );
}
```

- **Tabs** use the `Tabs` layout, or the platform tab bar from `expo-router/native-tabs` (SDK 58; SDK 54 to 57 used `expo-router/unstable-native-tabs`).
- **Modals** are ordinary routes presented with `presentation: 'modal'` or `'formSheet'`. Sheets take `sheetAllowedDetents` (ascending fractions from 0 to 1, or `'fitToContents'`) and `sheetInitialDetentIndex`.
- On **web** a modal is a separate route that a user can open directly, so a close button should check `router.canGoBack()` before calling `router.back()` and otherwise navigate to a sensible fallback.

Since SDK 56 expo-router no longer depends on react-navigation, so direct `@react-navigation/*` imports may break; use what expo-router exports.

Universal links (iOS) and App Links (Android) open the app for normal `https` URLs of a domain you control, landing on the matching route.

```json
{
  "expo": {
    "ios": { "associatedDomains": ["applinks:example.com"] },
    "android": {
      "intentFilters": [{
        "action": "VIEW",
        "autoVerify": true,
        "data": [{ "scheme": "https", "host": "example.com", "pathPrefix": "/records" }],
        "category": ["BROWSABLE", "DEFAULT"]
      }]
    }
  }
}
```

**iOS**
- `associatedDomains` entries are `applinks:<host>` with **no** `https://`.
- Serve the AASA file over HTTPS at `/.well-known/apple-app-site-association` (with expo-router web output, put it in `public/.well-known/`). Its `appID` is `"<TEAMID>.<BUNDLEID>"` and it lists `paths` or `components`.
- iOS fetches the file at install and update and "does not refresh frequently after that", so reinstall to test changes.

**Android**
- Serve `/.well-known/assetlinks.json` as `application/json` with `delegate_permission/common.handle_all_urls` and the **signing certificate's** `sha256_cert_fingerprints`.
- Take the fingerprint from `eas credentials -p android` or, with Play App Signing, from Play Console > App Signing "Digital Asset Links JSON". A local debug key's fingerprint will not verify the store build.
- Verification can take 20 seconds or more after install.

A link is untrusted input: validate params on the landing screen and keep it behind the same protected group as in-app navigation.

On the web the library listens to browser focus and online events. React Native has neither, so the TanStack React Native guide wires them up:

```ts
import NetInfo from '@react-native-community/netinfo';
import { focusManager, onlineManager } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { AppState, Platform, type AppStateStatus } from 'react-native';

// Reconnect: tell Query when the device comes back online
onlineManager.setEventListener((setOnline) =>
  NetInfo.addEventListener((state) => setOnline(!!state.isConnected))
);

// Focus: treat "app became active" as window focus
function onAppStateChange(status: AppStateStatus) {
  if (Platform.OS !== 'web') focusManager.setFocused(status === 'active');
}
// in a root component:
// useEffect(() => { const sub = AppState.addEventListener('change', onAppStateChange); return () => sub.remove(); }, []);
```

`expo-network` works in place of NetInfo. **Screen** focus is a separate concern: use `useFocusEffect` to refetch when a screen regains focus (skip the first call, which fires on mount), and pass `subscribed: false` together with `useIsFocused` to stop off-screen screens from re-rendering on cache updates.

Query keys are arrays that describe the data: `['todos']`, `['todo', id]`, `['todos', { status }]`. After a write, invalidate the keys whose data changed so active queries refetch:

```ts
const qc = useQueryClient();
const addTodo = useMutation({
  mutationFn: (title: string) => api.addTodo(title),
  onSettled: () => qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['todos'] }),
});
```

Return the promise from `invalidateQueries` in `onSettled` if you want the mutation to stay pending until the refetch completes.

For a snappy UI, write the expected result into the cache first and roll back on failure:

```ts
useMutation({
  mutationFn: api.toggleLike,
  onMutate: async (postId) => {
    await qc.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['post', postId] }); // stop an in-flight refetch overwriting us
    const previous = qc.getQueryData(['post', postId]);     // snapshot
    qc.setQueryData(['post', postId], (p: Post) => ({ ...p, liked: !p.liked }));
    return { previous };                                     // handed to onError / onSettled
  },
  onError: (_err, postId, result) => qc.setQueryData(['post', postId], result?.previous),
  onSettled: (_d, _e, postId) => qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['post', postId] }),
});
```

The order matters: cancel, snapshot, write, return the snapshot; restore it in `onError`; re-sync with the server in `onSettled`. Whatever `onMutate` returns is passed to `onError` and `onSettled` (v5 calls it the `onMutate` result).

Feeds use `useInfiniteQuery`. You give it the first page param and a function that derives the next one from the last page:

```ts
const feed = useInfiniteQuery({
  queryKey: ['feed'],
  queryFn: ({ pageParam }) => api.feed({ cursor: pageParam }),
  initialPageParam: null as string | null,
  getNextPageParam: (lastPage) => lastPage.nextCursor ?? undefined, // undefined = no more pages
});

<FlashList
  data={feed.data?.pages.flatMap((p) => p.items)}
  onEndReached={() => { if (feed.hasNextPage && !feed.isFetchingNextPage) feed.fetchNextPage(); }}
  ...
/>
```

Guarding with `hasNextPage` and `!isFetchingNextPage` stops the end-of-list callback from firing duplicate fetches. Keep pages in the query cache rather than concatenating them into component state, so refetch and invalidation keep working.

`expo-sqlite` is the choice for records you filter, sort, join or sync.

```ts
const db = await SQLite.openDatabaseAsync('app.db');
await db.execAsync('PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;');
const row = await db.getFirstAsync<{ user_version: number }>('PRAGMA user_version');
if ((row?.user_version ?? 0) < 1) {
  await db.execAsync('CREATE TABLE notes (id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, body TEXT, version INTEGER); PRAGMA user_version = 1;');
}
await db.runAsync('INSERT INTO notes (id, body, version) VALUES (?, ?, ?)', id, body, 1);
const notes = await db.getAllAsync<Note>('SELECT * FROM notes WHERE body LIKE ?', '%' + q + '%');
```

- Bind parameters (`?` or named) instead of building SQL strings; prepared statements escape them. Call `finalizeAsync` on a prepared statement in a `finally` block.
- Track schema version with `PRAGMA user_version` and run migrations in order. Enable WAL.
- `withTransactionAsync` "is not exclusive": other queries can interleave. `withExclusiveTransactionAsync` uses a separate connection, so connection-level settings such as `PRAGMA foreign_keys` do not apply inside it.
- `SQLiteProvider` (with `onInit` for migrations) and `useSQLiteContext` share one database across the tree.
- For encryption at rest, enable SQLCipher (`useSQLCipher`) and set `PRAGMA key`. Check `PRAGMA cipher_version`: without SQLCipher the key pragma **silently does nothing**.
- libSQL support was removed in SDK 58.

Define the rules once and derive the type from them:

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

export const SignUp = z.object({
  email: z.email('Enter a valid email'),
  password: z.string().min(8, 'Use at least 8 characters'),
});
export type SignUpValues = z.infer<typeof SignUp>;

const result = SignUp.safeParse(values);
if (!result.success) {
  const { fieldErrors } = z.flattenError(result.error); // { email?: string[]; password?: string[] }
  setErrors(fieldErrors);
} else {
  submit(result.data);
}
```

- `safeParse` returns `{ success, data }` or `{ success: false, error }`; `parse` throws. Use `safeParse` in event handlers so invalid input is a normal branch, not an exception.
- Show errors after a field is touched (on blur) or on submit, not on the first keystroke.
- Share the schema with the server if you can, but **always validate again on the server**: the client check is for user experience, not for security.

With `onlineManager` wired to network state, a mutation fired while offline is **paused**. Paused mutations "will be retried in the same order when the device reconnects".

If the app is killed while offline, the queue only survives if you persist the cache (for example with a query persister) **and** the mutations can be rebuilt after reload. A restored mutation has no function attached, so register a default per mutation key, then resume:

```ts
queryClient.setMutationDefaults(['addNote'], {
  mutationFn: (note: NoteInput) => api.addNote(note),
});

// after the persisted cache has been restored:
queryClient.resumePausedMutations();
```

Mutations that share a `scope: { id }` "will run in serial", so edits to the same record cannot overtake each other.

- **`keyExtractor`** should return a stable, unique id from the data (not the index), so React can keep each row's identity when items are inserted or removed.
- **Memoise rows.** Wrap the row component in `memo` and pass a `useCallback` `renderItem` so a parent re-render does not re-render every visible row.
- **Keep rows light.** Fewer nested views and less logic per row mean faster batches.
- **`getItemLayout`** for fixed-height rows lets the list compute offsets without measuring rendered items, which speeds rendering and makes `scrollToIndex` precise.

```tsx
const ITEM_HEIGHT = 72;
const getItemLayout = (_: unknown, index: number) => ({
  length: ITEM_HEIGHT,
  offset: ITEM_HEIGHT * index,
  index,
});
```

Reanimated's docs describe **worklets** as short-running JavaScript functions that can run on the UI thread. You read and write **shared values** through `.value`, and they stay in sync between the JS and UI threads. `useAnimatedStyle` turns shared values into styles computed on the UI thread, and helpers such as `withTiming` and `withSpring` animate them.

```tsx
const offset = useSharedValue(0);
const style = useAnimatedStyle(() => ({
  transform: [{ translateX: offset.value }],
}));
// later, from a handler:
offset.value = withTiming(200);
```

Current setup facts (Reanimated 4):

- It works only on the New Architecture (the only architecture in current React Native and Expo).
- It requires the separate `react-native-worklets` package; install both with `npx expo install`.
- The Babel plugin is `react-native-worklets/plugin` and must be listed last. In Expo, `babel-preset-expo` configures it automatically, so do not replace the preset with a hand-rolled config.
- Hermes is the default engine, so debug with React Native DevTools rather than older remote-debugging workflows.

Some users turn on Reduce Motion because large movement causes discomfort. Check it with `AccessibilityInfo.isReduceMotionEnabled()` and keep listening, because users can change it while the app runs:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  AccessibilityInfo.isReduceMotionEnabled().then(setReduce);
  const sub = AccessibilityInfo.addEventListener('reduceMotionChanged', setReduce);
  return () => sub.remove();
}, []);
```

When it is on, replace parallax, large slides and auto-playing motion with fades or instant changes. Test with VoiceOver and TalkBack on real devices; an automated check cannot tell you whether the reading order makes sense.

Apple has required approved reasons for "required reason" APIs on upload since 2024-05-01. The API categories include UserDefaults, file timestamps, system boot time, disk space and active keyboard. Apple emails you about missing reasons after an upload.

In an Expo app, declare them in app config under `ios.privacyManifests`:

```json
{
  "expo": {
    "ios": {
      "privacyManifests": {
        "NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes": [
          {
            "NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType": "NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults",
            "NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons": ["CA92.1"]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The Expo guide explains why you sometimes need to copy a dependency's reasons into app config: Apple "does not correctly parse all the PrivacyInfo files included by static CocoaPods dependencies". Separately, SDKs on Apple's third-party list (it includes hermes and Firebase pods) must ship their own manifest, and binary SDKs must also be signed.

Expo documents Jest with the `jest-expo` preset, which mocks the native parts of the Expo SDK so tests run in Node:

```json
{
  "jest": { "preset": "jest-expo" }
}
```

Install the testing library with `npx expo install @testing-library/react-native --dev`. Some packages in `node_modules` ship untranspiled code; `transformIgnorePatterns` takes a **regular expression** that controls which packages Jest leaves untransformed, so you widen it when a dependency fails with a syntax error. `collectCoverage: true` turns on coverage reports.

A component test renders the screen and queries it the way a user would, by visible text or accessibility information, not by internal state:

```tsx
import { render } from '@testing-library/react-native';
import HomeScreen from '../app/index';

test('shows the welcome message', async () => {
  const { getByText } = await render(<HomeScreen />);
  expect(getByText('Welcome!')).toBeTruthy();
});
```

The current Expo docs example **awaits** `render`, which matches RNTL 14. Check the current docs rather than memory when upgrading, because the preset and RNTL have both changed across recent releases.

MASVS-CODE-2 asks that the app "has a mechanism for enforcing app updates", which you need for security fixes and API retirements.

- **Android:** Google Play In-App Updates offers a **flexible** flow (download in the background while the user keeps working) and an **immediate** flow (full-screen, blocking until the update is installed). It requires Android 5.0+ and an install from Play, and does not work with `.obb` expansion files.
- **iOS:** there is no equivalent OS API. The common design is your own check: on launch the app fetches a **minimum supported version** from your backend and, if it is below, shows a blocking screen that links to the App Store listing.

```ts
const { minSupportedVersion } = await (await fetch(CONFIG_URL)).json();
if (isOlder(currentAppVersion, minSupportedVersion)) {
  showBlockingUpdateScreen(); // link to the store listing
}
```

Make the check fail open (let the app run) if the config endpoint is unreachable, or an outage of that one endpoint locks out every user. Note that an OTA update cannot satisfy a "new binary required" condition: it cannot change native code, permissions or the Expo SDK version.
